Z||000000||lowCG||04-09-2000||11:49 AM||||Well here's a couple pictures of the larger,Cadillac BALLJOINTS,(not spindles;oops) installed on my car after it's first,(and last!)Michigan winter.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.zing.com/album/?id=4294533203" TARGET=_blank>http://www.zing.com/album/?id=4294533203</A> <P>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 04-09-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 04-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-09-2000||12:10 PM||||Those are Caddy ball-joints with Fiero spindles, right?<P>Where did the "welding" part happen?<P>Roy||||reg||5
Z||000002||lowCG||04-09-2000||12:40 PM||||Yes,1975 CoupeDeVille,upper(there's a left,and a right #)but(just remembered)the flat retaining collar that's on the Moog brand Caddy joint needs to be cut off.This is very easy as the joint is only pressed through a 3/16 flat plate,which needs to be cut at it's thinnest poit,and removed(a few seconds with a Makita)leaving just the joint.<BR>This is how the Moog parts come,can't say about other brands,but used some on another car that had the mounting points for the Cadillac cast into the part,and these welded on just fine without removing anything.<P>The weld is on the lower,outside edge of the joint,and on the top face of the control arm,just around the hole that the old stock one was pressed into. <P>Some tape should be placed over the stud threads to ensure no weld spatter gets on the fine threads also.<P>I guarantee that there will be upset forum members who,after spending twice what stock balljoints cost,and paying someone to weld them on if the joint is welded all the way around before cooling with a spray of water.<BR>This is critical to mission success:ONLY weld a third of the way around the joint,after three small tack welds,then spray with a good amount of water,but don't submerge in water.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ozzy||04-09-2000||12:41 PM||||Are these all mods to improve pre '88's lowCG??<P>What did it "Fix" ?? and how much did it cost?<P>Ozzy<P>P.S. Now that I have this '87 GT, I'm sure bummed you are no longer in Tacoma dude 'cause I know it's gonna need some tweaking in the suspension dept.<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 04-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||04-09-2000||01:02 PM||||one mod,one procedure,for the early cars;never messed with the later cars,never owned one.<BR>Cost me a couple hundred bucks to get the parts and weld them to the control arms.<BR>The geometry change to the upper control arms helped the ride and reduced low speed understeer a little,while the spindle being raised up by the new,longer stud,lowered the car without having the lower control arm pointing up,altering the roll center,high speed stability.<BR>I wouldn't have done it twice if it didn't seem like a worthy idea <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spyder||04-09-2000||01:06 PM||||Thanks lowCG for the pics and the extra information.<P>I will be ordering the ball joints Monday morning.<BR>I will try to take pics of the installation.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||04-09-2000||09:29 PM||||Uh,yeah,just caught that spindle thing,the mod involves Caddy balljoints,sorry,I was felling a little queezy earlier when I wrote it.<BR>I now have two engines on my garage floor after this afternoons grunt session,a grimy old 2.8,along with a shiny 3.4TDC.<BR>||||reg||10
